{"type":"assistant","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Both tests pass. Here's what I changed in `repo/search.py`:\n\n**Fixed the insertion-point bug.** The comparison was `sorted_list[mid] < x`, which collapses equal elements into the lower half and yields the *leftmost* insertion point. Switching to `<=` collapses equal elements into the upper half, yielding the *rightmost* insertion point — so repeated values insert after existing ones, matching `test_equal_inserts_after` and the intent stated in the comment.\n\n**Removed `old_insert_index`.** It was a legacy pass-through wrapper (`return insert_index(...)`) flagged \"Remove after migration\", and nothing imports it (only `insert_index` is used, by `repo/rows.py`). Dead code removed.\n\n**Cleaned up the comments.** Replaced the stale \"BUG:\" note with a proper docstring describing the contract and O(log n) behavior.\n\nThe final helper:\n\n```python\ndef insert_index(sorted_list, x):\n    \"\"\"Return the rightmost index at which ``x`` can be inserted to keep ``sorted_list`` sorted.\n\n    Equal elements are inserted *after* the existing ones, so the returned\n    index is the count of elements strictly less than ``x`` plus the count of\n    elements equal to ``x``. Runs in O(log n) time.\n    \"\"\"\n    lo, hi = 0, len(sorted_list)\n    while lo < hi:\n        mid = (lo + hi) // 2\n        if sorted_list[mid] <= x:\n            lo = mid + 1\n        else:\n            hi = mid\n    return lo\n```\n\n`repo/rows.py`'s `bisect_summary` still imports `insert_index` and is unaffected by the change (it now reports the rightmost position, consistent with the fix)."}]},"session_id":"e34f1b4e-8f44-4e0d-958e-5ad01d576060"}
{"type":"result","subtype":"success","duration_ms":47005,"duration_api_ms":47005,"is_error":false,"result":"The bug is clear: `insert_index` uses `< x`, which yields the leftmost insertion point, but the test and the docstring comment expect the rightmost (equal elements should insert after existing ones). There's also a dead legacy wrapper `old_insert_index` that isn't imported anywhere. Let me clean it up.Now let me verify the fix against the tests:Both tests pass. Here's what I changed in `repo/search.py`:\n\n**Fixed the insertion-point bug.** The comparison was `sorted_list[mid] < x`, which collapses equal elements into the lower half and yields the *leftmost* insertion point. Switching to `<=` collapses equal elements into the upper half, yielding the *rightmost* insertion point — so repeated values insert after existing ones, matching `test_equal_inserts_after` and the intent stated in the comment.\n\n**Removed `old_insert_index`.** It was a legacy pass-through wrapper (`return insert_index(...)`) flagged \"Remove after migration\", and nothing imports it (only `insert_index` is used, by `repo/rows.py`). Dead code removed.\n\n**Cleaned up the comments.** Replaced the stale \"BUG:\" note with a proper docstring describing the contract and O(log n) behavior.\n\nThe final helper:\n\n```python\ndef insert_index(sorted_list, x):\n    \"\"\"Return the rightmost index at which ``x`` can be inserted to keep ``sorted_list`` sorted.\n\n    Equal elements are inserted *after* the existing ones, so the returned\n    index is the count of elements strictly less than ``x`` plus the count of\n    elements equal to ``x``. Runs in O(log n) time.\n    \"\"\"\n    lo, hi = 0, len(sorted_list)\n    while lo < hi:\n        mid = (lo + hi) // 2\n        if sorted_list[mid] <= x:\n            lo = mid + 1\n        else:\n            hi = mid\n    return lo\n```\n\n`repo/rows.py`'s `bisect_summary` still imports `insert_index` and is unaffected by the change (it now reports the rightmost position, consistent with the fix).","session_id":"e34f1b4e-8f44-4e0d-958e-5ad01d576060","request_id":"ef43a460-0ffc-4952-b940-b044586956ce","usage":{"inputTokens":10634,"outputTokens":1492,"cacheReadTokens":134304,"cacheWriteTokens":0}}
